Hello all.

First off, I'm an admin, not a developer so what I may be able to offer
in helping to resolve this is limited.

I've never been able to get ACPI to work on one particular system I have,
a Tyan 1834D SMP P3 motherboard.  It is now an issue that I must try to 
resolve, so here goes.  Using any kernel I've ever had on this box, be it
2.4.0 - 2.4.18, even 2.4.19 with the 9-28-02 ACPI patch, or 2.5.39... ACPI
will _always_ hang the sytem on boot if it is enabled, boots fine with acpi=
off, and pci=noacpi still hangs, but it seems to give different oopses sometimes.

Anything I can provide from this system, any further tests, patches, compile 
options etc. I'll be happy to provide.  Serial console kernel message dumps are
attached, to avoid a really long email for those who have the digest version.

Current symptoms are: 

___2.4.19 stock ACPI

ACPI enabled, kernel crashes badly during boot.  I haven't done a serial con.
capture on this kernel yet, but can provide it if needed. acpi=off works fine.

___2.4.19 w/9-28 ACPI patch- acpi enabled or pci=noacpi

ACPI seems to load correctly, but none of my disk interfaces seem to initialize
correctly.  My 2940U card comes up with an infinite number of looping SCSI bus 
errors (I have verbose scsi error reporting enabled). And if I remove the 2940
driver from the kernel, my 3Ware 6200 tries to send a reset 3 times then gives 
up and root can't be mounted.

___2.4.19 w/9-28 ACPI patch - with acpi=off , the system works OK in all respects.

___2.5.39 - ACPI enabled or pci=noacpi

With ACPI enabled, this is the end of the kernel messages then it hangs. There are also
oopses from what looks like the scheduler, probably because of the new code in 2.5.39.

Parsing Methods:...........................................................................
Table [DSDT] - 306 Objects with 26 Devices 75 Methods 19 Regions
ACPI Namespace successfully loaded at root c04e85bc

___2.5.39 - ACPI=off

The kernel boots, and I can sometimes get into X, but most of the time it hangs there.  
Some other issues, probably unrelated. More scheduler errors, and something from slab.c

That's it in a nutshell.  As I said I'll be more than happy to provide any information or
run any more tests/kernels/patches etc.  

Thanks for the ongoing work on ACPI for linux in general (it works great on everything else!:)
and specifically for any insight that is provided.

Cheers,

/eli




